 ## Background
-The shift from CPU-based ray casting to GPU implementations began with the advent of programmable graphics pipelines (e.g., DirectX 9, OpenGL Shading Language). Early implementations utilized multi-pass techniques to manage state and leverage early-z testing for early ray termination [7443-volume-ray-casting-on-graphics-hardware.md]. Subsequent developments focused on single-pass fragment shader implementations for real-time performance [5616-hardware-accelerated-volume-rendering.md], support for complex data structures like AMR [10503-hardware-accelerated-amr-volume-rendering-via-homogenization.md] and unstructured meshes [9631-gpu-based-tiled-ray-casting-with-depth-peeling.md], and web-based rendering via WebGL [8592-webgl-path-tracing-volume-rendering.md].
+The shift from software-based ray casting to hardware-accelerated implementations was driven by the need for real-time performance in medical imaging and scientific visualization. Early implementations leveraged the fixed-function texture hardware of GPUs to perform trilinear interpolation, which was significantly faster than CPU-based methods. The introduction of programmable shaders (fragment shaders) allowed the complex compositing logic and transfer function mapping to be executed in parallel for each pixel, enabling interactive frame rates. Subsequent developments addressed specific challenges such as multi-volume rendering, non-convex mesh handling via depth peeling, and adaptive mesh refinement (AMR) through homogenization. More recently, the field has expanded to include neural rendering techniques like NeRF-XL, which distribute neural network parameters across multiple GPUs for large-scale scene representation.
 
